# `**/target/`, not `target/`: the bare form matches only the repository root,
# and `tools/{indexer,watcher,mindexfile}` are separate crates with their own
# build directories. On a machine that has built them — which is any machine that
# has run the test matrix — that was ~5.5 GB of build context sent to the daemon
# on every build, for nothing: the image compiles from source inside itself.
**/target/
.git/
.claude/
tests/
docker-compose*.yml
*.md
.mypy_cache
.ruff_cache
# The same class of mistake in the other language ecosystems: none of this is in
# the image, and `node_modules` alone is tens of thousands of files.
**/node_modules/
**/dist/
**/out/
**/.venv*/
**/__pycache__/

# The two harnesses, and the same reasoning a third time. Neither is in the image
# and both keep working trees full of foreign source: `perf/corpus/.clones` holds
# five cloned GitHub repositories and `bench/.clones` holds four more, plus
# `bench/results` (654 MB for a full pass) and `bench/.data` (downloaded ground
# truth). Measured before this entry: **332 MB of build context** on a machine
# that had run both, sent to the daemon on every `docker compose build`.
#
# It matters more now than it did: the release workflow builds and pushes an image
# in CI, so this is also the difference between a checkout and a checkout plus
# whatever a benchmark left behind. The scripts themselves are excluded too — they
# are not in the image either, and a partial exclusion would leave the directory
# looking like it was meant to be there.
perf/corpus/
perf/results/
perf/plots/
bench/
